    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"This report presents information gathered in geologic reconnaissance of the Carolina slate belt and its immediate surroundings. Information is given in digital maps of 18 individual 1:100,000 quadrangles, a digital compilation of simplified geology at 1:250,000 scale, cross sections, spreadsheets containing data on rock types and structure observed at almost 5000 field stations, and this text.\r\n\r\nAmong lithotectonic units or belts of the southeastern Piedmont, the Carolina slate belt is anomalous, in that it is underlain by rocks of lower greenschist facies metamorphism that in general are only weakly deformed, in contrast to adjacent belts composed of complexly deformed high-grade metamorphic rocks and intrusive rocks. Regional units in the slate belt range in age from Late Proterozoic to Ordovician, but granites were intruded in Silurian-Devonian and Late Paleozoic times. Parts of the belt at the east side are covered by rocks deposited in a string of Mesozoic basins and by the onlap of Coastal Plain deposits. The oldest rocks in the slate belt occur in the northwest part, against the Milton belt. These make up a granite- greenstone complex that appears to be in continuity (across a border fault of minor displacement) with the Charlotte belt. Unconformably atop those rocks, and making up the eastern part of the belt through much of North Carolina, is the Virgilina sequence (informal term), consisting mostly of intermediate and felsic volcanic rocks, with local occurrences of clastic sedimentary rocks, and a few granitic intrusive bodies. The few zircon ages available date the Virgilina rocks at about 600 Ma.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
